= The Asturias Rising

With the CEDA's entry into the government, the UGT and the Socialist Youth call a general strike that becomes an armed insurrection in the mining valleys of Asturias. For two weeks, workers' committees hold much of the province, proclaiming a short-lived "red republic" in some mining towns. Scattered risings elsewhere - Catalonia briefly declares its own independent state - collapse within days.

The government's response is not the regular Army but the Army of Africa - Moorish regulars and Legion troops under General Franco's direction - who suppress the rising with a brutality that shocks even conservative opinion. Asturias is left occupied and embittered.

For the officers who led the campaign, the rising is proof of the Army's usefulness as a domestic weapon, and a rehearsal for a larger reckoning. For the left, it is proof that legal opposition may not be enough - and a martyrdom it will not forget when the Popular Front is formed.
